robin <usenet@unrated.net> wrote:
> Jim Miller wrote:
> ...
>> My broader point, though, is that it should matter little to us who
>> "owns" the name "nethack".  In practice and culture the game is
>> supported by many people, but under the present system ineffectually.
> ...
>
> Unfortunately, this is probably the least accurate thing you've said.
>
> It's _very_ important who owns the name NetHack; the folks on the
> DevTeam own it.  In my opinion, this is just obvious.

I disagree that it is important.

It might be important as long as there are user who haven't heard
about NetHackImproved. But as soon as the user hears that there is a
new NetHack variant out there that is just like NetHack but
addressing all the shortcomings of NetHack he can judge for himself.

At that point we got "competition". As far as competition can work
with games. The dynamics of game development and popularity are a bit
different then with normal application.

> Any other package, regardless of whether it originates as a fork of
> NetHack itself, is _not_ NetHack; it's something else.  Common usage
> in the open source world would certainly allow a forked project to use
> its parent project's name as _part_ of its own name, but certainly not
> to claim the original name for its own.

This is wrong. The NetHack license does not force anybody to change
the name of the program. Few licenses do this. For example the LaTeX
Project Public License does but that is more out of the necessity of
not providing incompatible LaTeX packages.

As long as the name of a program is not trademarked anybody can use it
the way they want.

Of course it would be not a very nice move to fork a project and not
rename it. But it could be done within the license. Otherwise even
porting NetHack to a different platform without changing the name
would not be legal.

> NetHack is NetHack; anything else is not.  ;-)

Are you talking about version 1.3d or 3.0.10 or 3.1.3 or 3.2.3 or
3.4.3 or ...

They are all quite different games.

> -robin
>
> (On the other hand, I'm all in favor of forks and variants; the more
> the merrier, I think.  I'm just responding here to what seems to be a
> claim that a forked project would have any right to call itself
> "NetHack".)

They could call it NetHack+. ;-)

Well a problem is also when to call a patch to NetHack a fork and when
not.

SlashEm started as patches to NetHack but now they are a different
game although still nethackish.

When does a patch become a fork?

As I understand it PatchHack doesn't consider itself as something
different although it has as many or more patches than the first
release of SlashEm.

Where do you draw the line? Is NetHack patched with the Lethe-patch
just a slightly or "LetheNetHack"? It is for sure not The Official
NetHack.

